Three versions of a composite data. Three versions of a composite dataset between the Economist and Harvard Dictionary were made to test if results were sensitive to how the data was processed how the denominator for the sentiment ratios was defined. The first version uses all words that were found in the OCR, the second removes common grammatical words (stopwords) like the, and, or, so, if, this, that, so, and etc, and the final one only includes words that appear in the Harvard Dictionary.  Comparing the three versions helps provide a robustness check that can help show if any patterns are caused by data-processing decisions or a meaningful shift in language use.
